//
// Found with loft#1700 (`@FR-B-Scope`, `@FR-L-CapRef`'s capture context).  A name more than one
// binding spells — a second `for e` loop (`e#1`, loft#915), a bind after the first one's block
// ended (loft#1700), a pattern's name — is several variables, and the spelling names one of
// them at a time.  The capture context offered every variable under its OWN name, so a closure
// capturing `e` took the FIRST binding's type while the value came from the live one: the
// record read an integer slot as text, an interpreter panic (`index out of bounds` in the store)
// and a native type error, on every tree.  A capture now offers each spelling at the binding it
// currently names (`Function::all_names_and_types`).
//
// @falsified-at: 5ba2b5ef5 — interpret exit 101 -> 0, interpret panicked -> clean, native exit 1 -> 0
// @falsified-by: tests/falsified/1700b-a-closure-captures-the-binding-its-name-spells-now.patch — interpret exit 101 -> 0, interpret panicked -> clean, native exit 1 -> 0
// CHANNEL: the panic (interpreter) / the compile (native) on the control tree.
// ARMED: every run.
// WITNESS: `index out of bounds` in `allocation.rs`.
// HOLDS: a closure over a name bound once (the closure corpus).

fn main() {
  n = 0;
  for e in [1, 2] { n += e; }
  out = "";
  for e in ["a", "b"] {
    f = fn(s: text) -> text { "{e}{s}" };
    out += f("y");
  }
  assert(out == "ayby" && n == 3, "a closure over a second loop's variable: {out} {n}");
  for p in [10, 20] { g = fn(k: integer) -> integer { p + k }; n += g(1); }
  assert(n == 35, "the first loop's variable, captured: {n}");
  println("a closure captures the binding its name spells now: every cell holds");
}
